     "type": "intro",
     "title": "When 'Great' Companies Later Fail",
     "content": "Good to Great was published in 2001, built on stock performance data running through the mid-1990s. Looking at what happened to the eleven featured companies in the years since publication complicates the picture in ways worth confronting directly, in the same spirit of brutal honesty the book itself recommends. Circuit City, one of the eleven original good-to-great companies, filed for bankruptcy in 2008 and was fully liquidated in 2009, disappearing from the market it had once been credited with mastering. Fannie Mae, another of the eleven, was placed into US government conservatorship in September 2008 amid a mortgage-related solvency crisis and accounting problems, effectively ending its era as an independently celebrated success story. Several other companies from the original list faded from market prominence, were acquired, or saw their competitive position erode substantially in the following two decades.\n\nThis is not a minor footnote. If 'greatness,' as the book operationally defines it - a specific, sustained, multi-year pattern of market-beating stock performance - can evaporate within less than a decade of being certified, it raises a serious question about what the identified traits actually predict. Were Level 5 leadership, the Hedgehog Concept, and a culture of discipline genuine causes of durable excellence, or were they patterns that happened to correlate with a particular window of success that had other, unexamined causes, and that offered no protection against what came after? This capstone lesson uses that uncomfortable follow-up record as an entry point into a broader methodological idea: survivorship bias, and the limits of any research that looks backward from known winners.",
