     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Survivorship Bias, Defined and Applied",
     "content": "Survivorship bias is the systematic error of drawing conclusions from a group that made it through some selection process - survived, succeeded, was included in a study - while ignoring or undercounting the larger group that didn't, and as a result overestimating how much the survivors' shared traits actually explain their success. A classic illustration comes from World War Two aircraft-loss analysis: engineers initially wanted to reinforce the parts of returning bomber planes that showed the most bullet holes, until a statistician pointed out that the returning planes were exactly the ones that could survive damage there - the planes hit in other locations weren't coming back to be studied at all. The lesson generalizes far beyond aircraft: any dataset built only from survivors will make surviving traits look protective even when they aren't, because the failures that lacked those traits are invisible to the analysis.\n\nApplied to Good to Great, the underlying research design selected eleven companies precisely because they had already achieved a known, specific outcome - a rare stock-performance pattern - and then searched backward for what those eleven companies had in common. That is a form of selecting on the outcome, sometimes called selecting on the dependent variable, and it has a specific and well-documented limitation: it can identify a shared correlate of the eleven successes, but it structurally cannot rule out that other companies, with none of the same traits, might have succeeded too by other paths, or that some companies with very similar traits simply got unlucky and failed. The truer test of the traits' predictive power is not whether they describe past winners, but whether they would have correctly forecast who succeeds next, and the subsequent record of Circuit City and Fannie Mae suggests the traits, whatever else they capture, are not a guarantee against future failure.",
