     "type": "example",
     "title": "Circuit City: Same Traits, Different Ending",
     "content": "Circuit City is the most direct case study for this problem, because the book credits it with the same traits - a disciplined culture, a clear Hedgehog Concept in consumer electronics retail, strong leadership transitions - that supposedly explained its rise, and yet those traits did not prevent its collapse roughly a decade later. Several specific decisions in the 2000s are commonly cited in the business press as contributing factors: in 2007, Circuit City laid off thousands of its more experienced, higher-paid sales associates and replaced many of them with lower-cost new hires, a decision that eroded the sales-floor expertise customers had come to rely on; the company also expanded aggressively into big-box square footage at a moment when Best Buy was executing a stronger big-box strategy and online retailers, especially Amazon, were beginning to erode electronics retail margins generally. By the time of its 2008 bankruptcy filing, competitive and structural pressures had combined with these internal decisions to overwhelm whatever cultural advantages the company had built earlier.\n\nFannie Mae's story points to a related but distinct limitation: its 2008 crisis was tied heavily to systemic mortgage-market dynamics, government-sponsored-enterprise-specific financial exposure, and accounting controversies - factors substantially outside the internal culture, leadership, and strategy variables the original research was designed to measure at all. In both cases, the lesson is not that Collins's traits were fabricated or meaningless during the study period; it's that having those traits at one point in time is not a permanent inoculation, and the original research design had no mechanism for detecting or predicting failures that would occur through decisions or dynamics arising well after the study window closed.",
